<h2 class="tex-section" id="section:Ind-Objects"><span data-tag="0639">9.2</span> Finitary Functors and Compact Objects</h2>
<p>
Let $R$ be an associative ring. Recall that a (left) $R$-module $M$ is <em>finitely presented</em> if it can be realized as a quotient $R^{n} / K$, where $K \subseteq R^ n$ is a finitely generated submodule. This condition admits a purely categorical reformulation: 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-proposition" id="063A">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/063A">Proposition <span data-tag="063A">9.2.0.1</span>.</a> Let $R$ be an associative ring and let $M$ be a left $R$-module. Then $M$ is finitely presented if and only if the functor </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \{  \textnormal{$R$-modules} \}  \rightarrow \operatorname{Set}\quad \quad N \mapsto \operatorname{Hom}_{R}(M,N) \]
</div>
<p> preserves small filtered colimits. </p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Assume first that $M$ is finitely presented: that is, there exists an exact sequence of left $R$-modules 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  R^{m} \xrightarrow {f} R^{n} \rightarrow M \rightarrow 0. \]
</div>
<p>
       For any filtered diagram of $R$-modules $\{  N_ i \} $ having colimit $N$, we have a commutative diagram of exact sequences 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=40pt{ 0 \ar [r] &amp;  \varinjlim _{i} \operatorname{Hom}_{R}(M, N_ i) \ar [r] \ar [d] &amp;  \varinjlim _{i} \operatorname{Hom}_{R}( R^{n}, N_ i ) \ar [d]^{\sim } \ar [r] &amp;  \varinjlim _{i} \operatorname{Hom}_{R}( R^{m}, N_ i) \ar [d]^{\sim } \\ 0 \ar [r] &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{R}(M, N) \ar [r] &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{R}( R^{n}, N) \ar [r] &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{R}( R^{m}, N). } \]
</div>
<p>
       Since the middle and right vertical maps are bijective, the left vertical map is also bijective. 
    <p>
      We now prove the converse. Let $\{  M_{\alpha } \} $ be the collection of all finitely generated submodules of $M$, partially ordered by inclusion. Then $M$ is the colimit of the filtered diagram $\{  M_{\alpha } \} $. If the functor $\operatorname{Hom}_{R}(M, \bullet )$ preserves small filtered colimits, then the tautological map 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \varinjlim _{\alpha } \operatorname{Hom}_{R}(M, M_{\alpha } ) \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{R}(M, M) \]
</div>
<p>
       is a bijection. In particular, the identity map $\operatorname{id}_{M}: M \rightarrow M$ factors through a finitely generated submodule of $M$, so $M$ is generated by a finite collection of elements $x_1, x_2, \cdots , x_ n$. Let $R^{n}$ be the free $R$-module on generators $\widetilde{x}_1, \cdots , \widetilde{x}_{n}$, so there is surjective $R$-module homomorphism $f: R^{n} \twoheadrightarrow M$ satisfying $f( \widetilde{x}_ i ) = x_ i$ for $1 \leq i \leq n$. Let $K$ be the kernel of $f$, and let $\{  K_{\beta } \} $ be the collection of all finitely generated submodules of $K$, partially ordered by inclusion. If the functor $\operatorname{Hom}_{R}(M, \bullet )$ preserves small filtered colimits, then the tautological map 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \varinjlim _{\beta } \operatorname{Hom}_{R}( M, R^{n} / K_{\beta }) \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{R}(M, R^{n}/K ) = \operatorname{Hom}_{R}(M,M) \]
</div>
<p>
       is bijective. In particular, the identity map $\operatorname{id}_{M}$ factors as a composition 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[ M \xrightarrow {g} R^{n} / K' \twoheadrightarrow R^{n} / K \simeq M, \]
</div>
<p>
       where $K'$ is some finitely generated submodule of $K$. Enlarging $K'$ if necessary, we may assume that $g$ carries each $x_ i$ to the image of $\widetilde{x}_ i$ in $R^{n} / K'$: that is, the composite map $g \circ f$ coincides with the tautological map from $R^{n}$ to $R^{n}/K'$. We then have $K = \ker (f) \subseteq \ker (g \circ f) = K'$, so that $K = K'$ is finitely generated and $M = R^{n} / K$ is finitely presented. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</p></p></p></p></p></p></p></p></p></p></p></article>
<p>
Proposition <a data-tag="063A" href="/tag/063A">9.2.0.1</a> motivates the following: 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-definition" id="063B">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/063B">Definition <span data-tag="063B">9.2.0.2</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be a category which admits small filtered colimits. We say that an object $C \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is <em>compact</em> if the functor $D \mapsto \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(C,D)$ preserves small filtered colimits. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="063C">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/063C">Remark <span data-tag="063C">9.2.0.3</span>.</a> Let $R$ be an associative ring and let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be the category of left $R$-modules. Proposition <a data-tag="063A" href="/tag/063A">9.2.0.1</a> asserts that an object $M \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is compact if and only if it is finitely presented as an $R$-module. This result has counterparts for many other classes of mathematical structures, which can be proved by the same argument: </p>
<p><ul>
<li><p>
Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}= \operatorname{Group}$ be the category of groups. An object $G \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is compact if and only if it is finitely presented as a group: that is, it can be realized as the quotient of a finitely generated free group by the normal subgroup generated by (all conjugates of) finitely many elements. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}= \mathrm{Ring}$ be the category of associative rings. Then an object $R \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is compact if and only if it is finitely presented as a ring: that is, it can be realized as the quotient of a noncommutative polynomial ring $\operatorname{\mathbf{Z}}\langle x_1, x_2, \cdots , x_ n \rangle $ by a finitely generated two-sided ideal. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}= \mathrm{CRing}$ be the category of commutative rings. Then an object $R \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is compact if and only if it is finitely presented as a commutative ring: that is, it can be realized as the quotient of a finitely generated polynomial ring $\operatorname{\mathbf{Z}}[x_1, \cdots , x_ n]$ by an ideal $I$ (here $I$ is automatically finitely generated, by virtue of the Hilbert basis theorem). 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-example" id="063D">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/063D">Example <span data-tag="063D">9.2.0.4</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}= \operatorname{Set}$ be the category of sets. An object $S \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is compact if and only if it is a finite set. See Example <a data-tag="064J" href="/tag/064J">9.2.5.9</a> for a more general statement. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-variant" id="063E">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/063E">Variant <span data-tag="063E">9.2.0.5</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}= \operatorname{Set_{\Delta }}$ be the category of simplicial sets. An object $S \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is compact if and only if it is a finite simplicial set, in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="0131" href="/tag/0131">3.6.1.1</a>. See Proposition <a data-tag="0137" href="/tag/0137">3.6.1.7</a>. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-example" id="063F">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/063F">Example <span data-tag="063F">9.2.0.6</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a topological space and let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be the category of open sets of $X$ (with morphisms given by inclusions). Then an object $U \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is compact if and only if $U$ is quasi-compact when viewed as a topological space: that is, every open cover of $U$ admits a finite subcover. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
In this section, we study an $\infty $-categorical counterpart of Definition <a data-tag="063B" href="/tag/063B">9.2.0.2</a>. Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category which admits small filtered colimits. Assume for simplicity that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is locally small, so that every object $C \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ corepresents a functor 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  h^{C}: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{S}}\quad \quad D \mapsto \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(C,D). \]
</div>
<p>
 We say that the object $C$ is <em>compact</em> if the functor $h^{C}$ preserves small filtered colimits (Definition <a data-tag="064A" href="/tag/064A">9.2.5.1</a>). In §<a data-tag="0649" href="/tag/0649">9.2.5</a>, we introduce several variants of this condition and study their formal properties. 
</p>
<p>
In practice, one can often characterize the compact objects of an ordinary category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ as those which can be built, using finite colimits, from very simple “generators” of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ (Remark <a data-tag="063C" href="/tag/063C">9.2.0.3</a>). For this reason, many authors use the term <em>finitely presentable object</em> for what we refer to as a <em>compact object</em>. Beware that, in the setting of $\infty $-categories, this terminology can be misleading. The collection of compact objects of an $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is closed under both finite colimits (Corollary <a data-tag="064Y" href="/tag/064Y">9.2.5.26</a>) and the formation of retracts (Remark <a data-tag="064Z" href="/tag/064Z">9.2.5.18</a>). In classical category theory, the latter is a special case of the former: any retract of an object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ can be realized as the coequalizer of a pair of morphisms $(e, \operatorname{id}_{X}): X \rightarrow X$ (see Corollary <a data-tag="03ZL" href="/tag/03ZL">8.5.2.5</a>). In the $\infty $-categorical setting, this is no longer true. Consequently, to construct a general compact object $C \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ out of “generators”, it is often necessary to allow both finite colimits <em>and</em> retracts. In §<a data-tag="067N" href="/tag/067N">9.2.6</a>, we study this phenomenon in the case where $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}= \operatorname{\mathcal{S}}$ is the $\infty $-category of spaces. Let us say that a Kan complex $X$ is <em>essentially finite</em> if there exists a weak homotopy equivalence $K \rightarrow X$, where $K$ is a finite simplicial set (Definition <a data-tag="067P" href="/tag/067P">9.2.6.1</a>). Then: 
</p>
<ul>
<li><p>
A Kan complex $X$ is essentially finite if and only if it it belongs to the smallest full subcategory $\operatorname{\mathcal{S}}_{\mathrm{fin}} \subseteq \operatorname{\mathcal{S}}$ which contains $\Delta ^0$ and is closed under finite colimits (Proposition <a data-tag="067R" href="/tag/067R">9.2.6.3</a>). 
</p>
</li><li><p>
Every essentially finite Kan complex is compact when viewed as an object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{S}}$ (Corollary <a data-tag="067U" href="/tag/067U">9.2.6.5</a>). 
</p>
</li><li><p>
In general, an object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{S}}$ is compact if and only if it can be realized as a retract of an essentially finite Kan complex (Proposition <a data-tag="067X" href="/tag/067X">9.2.6.7</a>). If this condition is satisfied, we say that $X$ is <em>finitely dominated</em> (Definition <a data-tag="067W" href="/tag/067W">9.2.6.6</a>). Beware that there exist finitely dominated Kan complexes which are not essentially finite: this is the subject of the classical <em>Wall finiteness obstruction</em> (see <span class="cite">[<a href="/bibliography/MR0211402">MR0211402</a>]</span>). 
</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>
 All of these results have counterparts where we replace $\operatorname{\mathcal{S}}$ by the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$ of (small) $\infty $-categories, which we formulate and prove in §<a data-tag="0685" href="/tag/0685">9.2.7</a>. 
</p>
<p>
Our final goal in this section is to establish some stability properties of compactness under various categorical constructions. Suppose we are given a finite diagram of $\infty $-categories $\{  \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{\alpha } \} $, where each $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{\alpha }$ admits small filtered colimits and each of the transition functors $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{\alpha } \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{\beta }$ is finitary. In §<a data-tag="06F5" href="/tag/06F5">9.2.9</a>, we show that an object $C = \{  C_{\alpha } \} $ of the limit $\infty $-category $\varprojlim ( \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{\alpha } )$ is compact provided that each $C_{\alpha }$ is compact as an object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{\alpha }$ (Corollary <a data-tag="06F7" href="/tag/06F7">9.2.9.2</a>; beware that the converse is false in general). Using formal arguments, the proof reduces to the special case of diagrams of the form 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{-} \xrightarrow { F_{-} } \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\xleftarrow { F_{+} } \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{+}, \]
</div>
<p>
 in which case it can be regarded as an assertion about compact objects of the homotopy fiber product $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{-} \times ^{\mathrm{h}}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}} \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{+}$. We deduce this assertion in §<a data-tag="06EU" href="/tag/06EU">9.2.8</a> from a more general result about compact objects of an oriented fiber product $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{-} \vec{\times }_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}} \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{+}$ (see Proposition <a data-tag="06EV" href="/tag/06EV">9.2.8.3</a> and <a data-tag="06EX" href="/tag/06EX">9.2.8.5</a>), which has a number of other useful consequences. 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="06D9">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06D9">Remark <span data-tag="06D9">9.2.0.7</span>.</a> With an eye toward future applications, it will be useful to formulate the definitions and results of this section in greater generality. Suppose we are given a pair of regular cardinals $\kappa \leq \lambda $. We will say that an $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is <em>$(\kappa ,\lambda )$-cocomplete</em> if it admits colimits indexed by diagrams which are $\lambda $-small and $\kappa $-filtered (Definition <a data-tag="06DD" href="/tag/06DD">9.2.1.3</a>). If this condition is satisfied, we say that a functor $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ is <em>$(\kappa ,\lambda )$-finitary</em> if it preserves such colimits (Definition <a data-tag="062F" href="/tag/062F">9.2.2.6</a>). We say that an object $C \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is <em>$(\kappa ,\lambda )$-compact</em> if the corepresentable functor $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}( C, \bullet )$ is $(\kappa ,\lambda )$-finitary (Definition <a data-tag="064M" href="/tag/064M">9.2.5.12</a>). Following the convention of Remark <a data-tag="03PU" href="/tag/03PU">4.9.0.4</a>, we recover the usual notion of compact object by taking $\kappa = \aleph _0$ and $\lambda =  \Omega $ to be a strongly inaccessible cardinal. However, there are some other special cases worthy of note: for example, an $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $(\aleph _0, \aleph _1)$-cocomplete if and only if it is sequentially cocomplete, in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="06DD" href="/tag/06DD">9.2.1.3</a> (see Corollary <a data-tag="06EB" href="/tag/06EB">9.2.3.8</a>). In §<a data-tag="06E3" href="/tag/06E3">9.2.3</a>, we prove more generally that an $\infty $-category is $(\kappa , \kappa ^{+})$-cocomplete if and only if it admits colimits indexed by linearly ordered sets of order type $\kappa $ (and a functor is $(\kappa , \kappa ^{+})$-finitary if and only if it preserves such colimits); see Proposition <a data-tag="06E9" href="/tag/06E9">9.2.3.6</a>. </p>
</article>
</p>
